Phase 2: Server endpoints (2–3 days)

What: Give the app a small catalog snapshot it can store, and make the server the real gatekeeper of paid content.

Where: apps/api/src/modules/catalog, content, learning, assessment, plus packages/contracts/src and packages/database/prisma/schema.

How:

Catalog snapshot endpoint (new, for example GET /catalog/snapshot?since=<version>):
Returns only metadata: courses, topics, chapters, exam/test/COC set headers (id, title, order, type, isFree, courseId, updatedAt).
It never returns lesson bodies or questions.
The since parameter gives a delta: changed rows plus a list of deleted ids. This needs a soft-delete or tombstone column and an index on updatedAt.
Support ETag / If-None-Match, so a repeat call with no changes returns 304 with no body.
Paginate the first full sync (about 500 rows per page) and enable gzip.
Add the response shape to packages/contracts so mobile and admin share one type.
Body endpoints stay online: GET content/topics/:topicId and the assessment endpoints.
Verify that each one checks, on the server, that the student has paid or that the item is free. Do not trust the isFree flag from the client.
An unpaid student asking for a paid body must receive 403 with your existing error code (for example CONTENT_LOCKED).
Entitlement: Your signedEntitlement in packages/domain/src/entitlement already exists. Make home-summary or my-courses return it with the exact expiresAtMs. The app will use it offline.
Indexes: Add Postgres indexes on the foreign keys and updatedAt columns used by the snapshot query. Run EXPLAIN on it with realistic data.
Caching: Cache the snapshot per version in Redis (you already run local Redis). Invalidate the version when an admin publishes or edits content.
Protection: Add rate limits on the body and snapshot endpoints (for example, per student and per device). Apply validation to all parameters.

Done when: An unpaid test account gets 403 on every paid body endpoint, and the snapshot returns 304 when nothing changed.

Phase 3: Local catalog database on mobile (2 days)

What: A local copy of the lists, so screens can render at once, offline included.

Where: apps/mobile/src/core/db/migrations.ts (add version 4, never edit 1–3), and a new apps/mobile/src/core/sync/catalogSync.ts next to contentSync.ts.

How:

Migration v4 adds tables such as:
sql
CREATE TABLE catalog_item (
  user_id TEXT NOT NULL, id TEXT NOT NULL, kind TEXT NOT NULL,
  course_id TEXT NOT NULL, parent_id TEXT, title TEXT NOT NULL,
  position INTEGER NOT NULL, is_free INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,
  meta TEXT, updated_at INTEGER NOT NULL,
  PRIMARY KEY (user_id, id)
);
CREATE INDEX idx_catalog_list ON catalog_item (user_id, course_id, kind, parent_id, position);
CREATE TABLE catalog_state (user_id TEXT PRIMARY KEY, version TEXT, synced_at INTEGER);
catalogSync.ts calls the snapshot endpoint with the stored version, applies the changes inside one withTransactionAsync, then saves the new version. It never leaves a half-applied state.
When to run it:
After login and after course selection.
On every app start when online.
When the app returns to the foreground (throttled to once every few minutes).
When connectivity returns. Use the existing connectivity.ts and SyncProvider.tsx.
Retry with the backoff you already have in packages/domain/src/sync/backoff.ts.

Security: Metadata only, so a copied database exposes titles, never paid material. Done when: After one online sync, airplane mode still shows every course, topic and exam list.

Phase 4: Local-first screens and the single open rule (3 days)

What: Replace the download-first gates with the new access model.

Where:

packages/domain/src/access/ for the rule.
apps/mobile/src/core/downloads/contentGateway.ts for lists.
The hooks modules/courses/hooks/useTopics.ts and useCourseDetail.ts, and the exam, test and COC list hooks.
The components that check useIsCourseDownloaded: MaterialTab, TestsTab, ExamsTab, CourseCard, CourseActionButton and CourseScreen.

How:

One pure function in the domain package, for example resolveOpen({ access, isFree, isDownloaded, isOnline }), returning LOCKED, OPEN_LOCAL, OPEN_ONLINE or NEEDS_INTERNET. It wraps your existing canOpenContent. Test every row of the matrix, because both mobile and the server tests can reuse it.
Lists read from SQLite: useTopics and its siblings read catalog_item first and render immediately. If online, they refresh in the background and update only when data changed (stale-while-revalidate). No spinner when local rows exist.
Delete the if (!isDownloaded) branches and the “download first” prompt in the six files. Call resolveOpen on tap and act on the result:
LOCKED: show the existing “Locked Content” dialog with the Buy button to Store.
OPEN_LOCAL or OPEN_ONLINE: go to /reader/....
NEEDS_INTERNET: show a clear message, “Connect to the internet to open this, or download it when you are online.”
In contentGateway.ts, stop readContentList from returning only downloaded items offline. Lists now come from the catalog. CONTENT_NOT_DOWNLOADED applies only to bodies.
Access state (paid or not) comes from the cached entitlement through offlineAccess, so locks are correct offline.
Update copy in packages/i18n (English and Amharic) for the new messages.

Performance: Use indexed SQLite queries. For long lists switch to FlashList, or tune FlatList with getItemLayout, initialNumToRender and windowSize. Done when: Opening any list takes one frame with data on screen, and every row in the matrix is tested.

Phase 5: Opening bodies and optional downloads (2–3 days)

What: Online students open any allowed body without downloading. Offline students open only what they saved.

Where: core/downloads/downloadStore.ts, transfer.ts, modules/downloads, and the reader module.

How:

Online open: the reader fetches the body from the API and shows it. Do not write it to disk silently. Your migration v3 deliberately deleted silent copies, and that fits your rule that offline works only for downloaded content. Keep the body in memory only while the reader is open.
Latency while online: prefetch the next chapter body into memory after the current one renders, request gzip, and send only the fields the reader needs. Use KaTeX caching (below) so the first paint is fast.
Download becomes optional: keep the Download and Delete buttons. Downloading saves bodies into downloaded_content as it does today, and Delete removes only the body copies, never the catalog rows.
Offline reader: read from downloadStore.getContent. Before showing a paid body, re-check access with offlineAccess and the trusted clock.
Storage control: show the size per course (totalBytes exists), warn when storage is low, and let the student remove a course download. Keep the app under your 50 MB limit by not bundling content.
Integrity: download a unit in one transaction (you already do this in saveUnit), so a cancelled download never leaves a half unit that looks complete.

Done when: All five rows of the access table behave as specified on a real phone.

Phase 6: Security hardening (2 days)

Where and how:

Server is the authority. Phase 2, step 2, is the main control. The app’s lock screen is only the user interface.
Entitlement signing: verify the signature on the phone with the public key, and never trust an unsigned PAID flag in local storage.
Offline grace: OFFLINE_GRACE_DAYS is 7 today. Decide the right number for your students, because it is how long a refunded or expired student can keep paid downloads without reconnecting. Rolling back the device clock is already handled by trustedNowMs. Keep that.
Downloaded paid content: contentKeys.ts already separates the device key (free content) from the entitlement key (paid content). Verify that downloaded paid bodies are encrypted with the entitlement key, and that the key is removed when access ends.
Device lock: keep the one-account-one-device rule and the x-device-id header on every body request.
Screen protection: keep ContentProtection.tsx on the reader screens.
Admin side: confirm that every admin route checks the Admin role on the server, not only in the page middleware.
Secrets and uploads: run gitleaks, keep .env out of git, and move uploaded files out of apps/api/uploads (Phase 8).

Done when: Tests show that an unpaid account, an expired account, a different device and a rolled-back clock each fail to open paid content.

Phase 7: Performance (2 days)
Lists: FlashList, stable keys, memoized row components, and no inline styles created on each render.
KaTeX: core/math bundles KaTeX offline. Cache the generated markup per chapter in memory so reopening a chapter does not re-parse it, and render long chapters in sections.
Startup: run catalog sync after the first screen is drawn, never before it. Keep the splash short by reading the session from secure storage only.
SQLite: use the indexes from Phase 3, batch writes in transactions, and check with EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN on the list queries.
API: paginate every admin list (this is already done according to your notes), and check N+1 queries in the NestJS services with Prisma logging.
Bundle size: run an Expo bundle analysis and remove unused fonts and libraries.
Admin web: use next/dynamic for heavy screens such as the scan wizard and question editor.

Done when: You have measured numbers on a low-end Android phone (time to first list, time to first chapter) and they are acceptable to you.implement this clearly and quickly

Phase 8: Availability and resilience (2–3 days)

Mobile:

Every network call goes through the existing client with timeouts and backoff, and the outbox continues to hold writes made offline.
Show an offline banner and a last-synced time, so the student knows the data may be old.
Never crash on a sync failure. Fall back to the local catalog.

Server and infrastructure:

Health endpoints for the API and worker, and a readiness check that tests Postgres and Redis.
Run at least 2 API instances behind a load balancer if your host allows it, and use zero-downtime deploys.
Daily automatic PostgreSQL backups with a tested restore.
Move uploads to object storage or a persistent volume.
Error tracking (such as Sentry), structured logs, and alerts on error rate and slow queries.
Graceful degradation: if Redis is down the API still works from Postgres, just slower.
Run prisma migrate deploy in the release step, with a rollback plan.

Done when: You can stop the API for 10 minutes and the app still lists and opens downloaded content with no crash. implement this clearly and quickly

Phase 9: Testing and release (2–3 days)
Unit tests: resolveOpen matrix, snapshot delta apply, entitlement expiry, clock rollback.
API tests: 403 for paid bodies without payment, 304 on unchanged snapshot, tombstones.
Device tests with airplane mode, for each of these accounts: unpaid, paid, expired, paid-then-offline for 8 days.
Load tests: run the three k6 scripts in tests/load, plus one for the snapshot endpoint.
Staging: deploy there first with production-like data, and test the admin to mobile flow (publish, mobile sync, student sees the change).
Release: EAS production build, check APK size under 50 MB, staged rollout to a small group, then everyone.
Suggested order implement this clearly and quickly

Phases 0 → 2 → 3 → 4 → 5 give you the new behaviour. Phase 1 (colours) is independent, so you can run it in parallel. Phases 6 → 9 are the readiness work, and parts of 6 and 7 should be tested while you build 4 and 5.